                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ever wondered what it would be like to explore an abandoned, haunted apartment complex, just like television paranormal reality shows Ghost Adventures and Ghost Hunters (but without the annoying hosts and the camera from \"Cloverfield\")? Say no more, fam. Whet your [strike]pants[/strike] curiosity with this interesting horror piece that is The 9th Gate.\n\n[h1][b][u]About The Game[/u][/b][/h1]\nA girl with psychic abilities enters a creepy, abandoned apartment complex (armed only with a cell phone) in search of a missing little girl because, why not? In order to complete her mission, she is required to search for several creepy looking dolls scattered around the cursed complex.\n\nInspired by Asian horror movies such as the [b]Tales of Terror from Tokyo and All Over Japan, The Eye, Shutter and Ju-On[/b] (just to name a few), The 9th Gate brings to life many Asian horror folklore from South East Asia, Japan and other Asian countries. Unlike the Western horror genre filled with boring zombies, vampires and chain-saw wielding psychopaths, The 9th Gate introduces you to the dark side of Asian superstition, with disembodied voices and floating phantoms roaming the darkened hallways looking for their next unsuspecting victim.\n\n[h1][b][u]Gameplay - 8/10[/u][/b][/h1]\nYou control the protagonist in first-person. As weapons do jack squat towards the supernatural, you do not carry any except for a cell phone. The cell phone allows you to (slightly) illuminate the dark surroundings with its pathetic rear camera flash, as well as glancing the next creepy doll that needs to be located via the phone’s gallery. There is also a live camera mode, which allows you to see certain roaming ghosts so that you can avoid or scare them away (plus you get to see better in the dark). Another function is a speaker mode, which you'll need to use in order to locate the dolls as it gives you audible clues when you are near one. \n\nMoving around the complex while juggling your phone's functions can be a bit clunky at times (of panic) but there is also a Prologue chapter, which will help you acclimatise to the controls as well give you a brief backstory. There are also different difficulty levels if you find yourself a bit overwhelmed.\n\n[h1][b][u]Atmosphere - 10/10[/u][/b][/h1]\nNo horror game is complete without spooky ambient noises, and this game has it in spades. Random disturbances and eerie voices fill your ears every step of the way. Headphones are recommended for the best experience. The graphics are also nice and immerses you in a hauntingly realistic environment, with dimmed hallways, abandoned rooms and derelict furniture lending to the eerie atmosphere. The wandering spirits will always keep you on your toes. Don’t worry about finding them, as they’ll be looking for you instead.\n\n[h1][b][u]Conclusion - RECOMMEND [/u][/b][/h1]\nThe 9th Gate is a scary game, both figuratively and literally, as there are still some bugs (though not game-breaking, but still rather annoying) that the dev will hopefully iron out in due time. However, look past those, and you get a game that provides a high level of immersion rarely seen in most horror games.\n\nSo, if you’re a fan of Asian horror movies or just looking for a game that threatens to strike you down with crippling panic attacks and make you sleep with the lights on for the next few weeks, then look no further.\n\n[h1][b][u]Pros:[/u][/b][/h1]\n[list]\n[*]Nice graphics and sound\n[*]Excellent atmosphere\n[*]Easy controls (once you get use to them)\n[*]Reasonably priced\n[/list]\n\n[h1][b][u]Cons: [/u][/b][/h1]\n[list]\n[*]Still a bit buggy\n[*]Sleepless nights\n[*]Soiled pants\n[/list]",
